# Inter-VLAN Routing (Basic)

### Objective
Design and configure a network with 3 VLANs on a single switch, enable inter-VLAN routing using a Cisco router (router-on-a-stick), and apply an Access Control List (ACL) to restrict traffic between VLANs.

### Topology
![Topology](./screenshots/vlan1.png)

```
Router0 (Cisco 2911)
└── Switch0 (Cisco 2960)
    ├── PC0 — VLAN 10 (IT)
    ├── PC1 — VLAN 20 (Staff)
    └── PC2 — VLAN 30 (Guest)
```

### Devices Used
| Device | Model | Count |
|--------|-------|-------|
| Router | Cisco 2911 | 1 |
| Switch | Cisco 2960 | 1 |
| PC | PC-PT | 3 |

### VLAN Plan
| VLAN | Name | Subnet | Gateway |
|------|------|--------|---------|
| 10 | IT | 192.168.10.0/24 | 192.168.10.1 |
| 20 | Staff | 192.168.20.0/24 | 192.168.20.1 |
| 30 | Guest | 192.168.30.0/24 | 192.168.30.1 |

### IP Address Plan
| Device | VLAN | IP Address | Gateway |
|--------|------|------------|---------|
| PC0 | 10 (IT) | 192.168.10.2 | 192.168.10.1 |
| PC1 | 20 (Staff) | 192.168.20.2 | 192.168.20.1 |
| PC2 | 30 (Guest) | 192.168.30.2 | 192.168.30.1 |

---

### Configuration Summary

**Switch — VLAN & Port Assignment:**
- Fa0/1 → VLAN 10 (IT) — access mode
- Fa0/2 → VLAN 20 (Staff) — access mode
- Fa0/3 → VLAN 30 (Guest) — access mode
- Gig0/1 → Router — trunk mode (carries all VLANs)

![VLAN Brief](screenshots/vlan2.png)

![Trunk Port](screenshots/trunk.png)

**Router — Router-on-a-Stick (subinterfaces on Gig0/1):**
- Gig0/1.10 → 192.168.10.1 — gateway for VLAN 10
- Gig0/1.20 → 192.168.20.1 — gateway for VLAN 20
- Gig0/1.30 → 192.168.30.1 — gateway for VLAN 30

![Router Interfaces](screenshots/router-interfaces.png)

![Routing Table](screenshots/routing-table.png)

**ACL — Guest VLAN restriction:**
```
access-list 100 deny ip 192.168.30.0 0.0.0.255 192.168.10.0 0.0.0.255
access-list 100 permit ip any any
interface gig0/1.30
ip access-group 100 in
```
Applied inbound on Gig0/1.30 — blocks Guest from reaching IT, allows everything else.

---

### Test Results
| Test | From | To | Expected | Result |
|------|------|----|----------|--------|
| Same VLAN | PC0 (VLAN 10) | PC0 gateway | Reachable | Pass |
| Inter-VLAN | PC0 (VLAN 10) | PC1 (VLAN 20) | Reachable |  Pass |
| Inter-VLAN | PC0 (VLAN 10) | PC2 (VLAN 30) | Reachable |  Pass |
| ACL block | PC2 (VLAN 30) | PC0 (VLAN 10) | Blocked |  Pass |
| ACL allow | PC2 (VLAN 30) | PC1 (VLAN 20) | Reachable |  Pass |

![Inter-VLAN Ping Success](screenshots/ping-intervlan-success.png)

![ACL Block](screenshots/ping-acl-blocked.png)

![ACL Allow](screenshots/ping-acl-allowed.png)

---

### What I Learned
- How VLANs segment a network at Layer 2 — devices in different VLANs cannot communicate without a Layer 3 device
- How router-on-a-stick works — one physical interface handles multiple VLANs using subinterfaces with 802.1Q encapsulation
- How trunk ports carry tagged traffic for multiple VLANs between a switch and router
- How to write and apply an ACL to restrict traffic between specific subnets
- How wildcard masks work in Cisco ACL syntax (`0.0.0.255` = entire /24 subnet)
- Why "Gateway of last resort is not set" is normal in an isolated lab with no internet uplink

